// Title: Suspicious TSCON Start as SYSTEM
// Sigma ID: 9847f263-4a81-424f-970c-875dab15b79b
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: Florian Roth (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2018-03-17 | Modified: 2022-05-27
// ATT&CK: command-and-control, T1219.002
// Description: Detects a tscon.exe start as LOCAL SYSTEM
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - http://www.korznikov.com/2017/03/0-day-or-feature-privilege-escalation.html
//   - https://medium.com/@networksecurity/rdp-hijacking-how-to-hijack-rds-and-remoteapp-sessions-transparently-to-move-through-an-da2a1e73a5f6
//   - https://www.ired.team/offensive-security/lateral-movement/t1076-rdp-hijacking-for-lateral-movement
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_tscon_localsystem.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (AccountName contains "AUTHORI" or AccountName contains "AUTORI") and FolderPath endswith "\\tscon.exe"
